    "narrative": "The core of the lineup today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who boasts our highest efficiency score at 87.14. Facing the hittable 4.96 xERA of Feltner, **Corbin Carroll** is positioned in the #2 hole with a significant boost from an elite platoon advantage, making him our priority for stolen base production. Joining him in the top tier are **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **Gabriel Moreno** (71.10). **Mickey Moniak** is a standout start today; his 14% boost from a dynamic platoon edge against the 5.14 xERA of Kelly, Me makes him a sneaky candidate for a multi-hit game. Similarly, **Isaac Paredes** (70.97) capitalizes on the struggling Houser, benefiting from a massive 18% skill-based matchup adjustment that elevates his ceiling despite a neutral park factor.\n\nOur most difficult decision was the final UTIL slot, where **Austin Riley** (58.74) narrowly edged out **Tyler Soderstrom** (57.87) for the primary roster. While **Tyler Soderstrom** carries a strong 57.86, he is forced to contend with a difficult matchup against the 3.17 xERA of Rasmussen, which dragged his base projection down significantly despite the hitter-friendly park factor. **Austin Riley** remains a volatile play given his spot in the #8 hole, but his ability to exploit the soft 4.62 xERA of Thornton keeps him above the marginal threshold. We are monitoring **Max Muncy** (42.29) and **Henry Bolte** (41.35) closely; both are hovering near the floor of our starting criteria due to the heavy defensive presence of Rasmussen. Should either be bumped from the lineup, we will look to rotate in depth pieces like **Zach McKinstry** or **Edwin Arroyo** if the daily utility projections shift. We continue to manage the health of **James Wood**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Giancarlo Stanton**, who remain sidelined on the IL, leaving our current offensive engine to carry the load in these high-leverage matchups.",
